    <details>
      <summary>Tax assumptions, exclusions and official sources</summary>
      <div class="details-copy">
        <div>
          <p><strong>What is included.</strong> The 2026/27 UK tax year, running from 6 April 2026 to 5 April 2027; employment Income Tax for England, Wales and Northern Ireland or Scotland; the standard £12,570 Personal Allowance and its taper above £100,000; and employee Class 1 National Insurance category A using monthly thresholds.</p>
          <p><strong>What is not included.</strong> Student or postgraduate loans, benefits in kind, tax-code adjustments, other income, Gift Aid, Marriage Allowance, Blind Person’s Allowance, High Income Child Benefit Charge or employer NI. Scottish bands apply here only to employment income.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p><strong>Method.</strong> Basic monthly pay, the hourly rate and each pay line are rounded to pennies to mirror the July payslip. Current taxable monthly pay is multiplied by 12, annual tax is calculated, then divided by 12. NI is calculated directly for the month at 8% between £1,048 and £4,189 and 2% above £4,189. Results are estimates unless actual PAYE or NI is entered.</p>
          <p><strong>Official sources.</strong></p>
          <ul class="source-list">
            <li><a href="https://www.gov.uk/income-tax-rates/current-rates-and-allowances" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">GOV.UK Income Tax rates and allowances</a></li>
            <li><a href="https://www.gov.uk/guidance/rates-and-thresholds-for-employers-2026-to-2027" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">GOV.UK 2026/27 PAYE and NI thresholds</a></li>
            <li><a href="https://www.gov.uk/scottish-income-tax" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">GOV.UK Scottish Income Tax</a></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
